
  <sn><md>### Xenobiotics and Endogenous Substances

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/media/toxicology/xenobiotics.jpeg)

**Xenobiotics:** Any `foreign chemical compound` introduced into an organism that is ++not naturally produced or expected++ to be present within its normal metabolic pathways (e.g., synthetic pesticides, halogenated industrial solvents, microplastics).

**Endogenous Substances:** Molecules that are `naturally synthesized` within the biological system (e.g., hormones, neurotransmitters, metabolic substrates like glutamate). 
Endogenous molecules can ++elicit++ toxicity if their `internal concentrations are dysregulated` or if endocrine-disrupting xenobiotics mimic their molecular structures and bind to their natural physiological receptors.</md></sn>
